{
  "requirement": "```rust,ignore\n/// Contents paired with the fingerprint of that same observation.\n#[derive(Clone, Debug)]\npub struct Snapshot<T> {\n    pub content: T,\n    pub fingerprint: Fingerprint,\n}\n```\n\nThis type lives in `habeas_sdk::graph`. For node reads, the SDK constructs it by\npassing the identity and the contents returned by `NodeContents::node_content` to\n`NodeContents::fingerprint`, without another read. For a label-specific edge\nquery, the SDK constructs the snapshot from the returned targets and their\ntarget-set fingerprint, without another query.\n`T` needs no filesystem path or source span.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests adequately check `requirements.sdk.graph.fingerprinting.snapshot`.\n\nI read the `Snapshot` implementation only to see how the construction paths are shaped. That is `InspectionContext::read_snapshot`, `prepare_read` and `prepare_edges` in `crates/habeas-sdk/src/graph.rs`. I took the expected behavior from the requirement text.\n\n**Node reads.** The requirement says the SDK builds the snapshot by passing the identity and the contents from `node_content` to `fingerprint`, with no further read.\n- `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/contracts.rs#fn[identities_remain_stable_and_fingerprinting_uses_the_supplied_snapshot]` calls `read_snapshot`. It then mutates the graph and asserts that `graph.fingerprint(&Node(1), &snapshot.content)` still equals `snapshot.fingerprint` and that its value is `\"before\"`. It also asserts the graph recorded exactly one read (`reads == [1]`). Together these show the content and fingerprint come from the same observation, with no extra read.\n- `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/validation.rs#fn[snapshots_record_exact_reads_and_retain_distinct_versions]` asserts the fingerprint of `read_snapshot` and the recorded `Dependency::Node` entries for the \"before\" and \"after\" versions. It also asserts one graph read per call (`reads == [1,1,1,2]`), so no extra read occurs.\n- `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/agent.rs#fn[graph_tools_record_delivered_snapshots_and_prepared_pages_do_not_record_delivery]` checks that the recorded fingerprint matches the delivered 18,000-character content.\n- `src/sdk.rs#fn[requirement_edges_and_input_freshness_use_the_sdk]` checks the snapshot content and fingerprint against a real domain. It confirms that fingerprinting a retained content yields the retained fingerprint even after the underlying source changes.\n\n**Label-specific edge queries.** The requirement says the snapshot is built from the returned targets and their target-set fingerprint, without another query.\n- `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/validation.rs#fn[labeled_selections_track_only_target_membership_without_reading_contents]` asserts the recorded `Dependency::OutgoingEdges` fingerprint equals `edge_fingerprint(&graph, &[3, 5])`. It also asserts that `successors` returns the targets as listed, `[5, 3, 5]`, and that no node contents are read.\n- `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/agent.rs#fn[agent_edge_tools_record_selections_without_validating_targets]` uses a graph whose edge lookup fails on any second call. It asserts `lookups == 1` and that the stored dependency carries the edge fingerprint, so no second query happens.\n- `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/validation.rs#fn[explicit_insertions_preserve_order_repetition_and_selection_snapshots]` checks the edge dependency fingerprint `edge_fingerprint(&graph, &[2, 3])` alongside the node snapshots.\n\n**Type shape and location.** All tests import `Snapshot` from the SDK graph module and use the `content` and `fingerprint` fields. The `Clone`/`Debug` derives and the \"no path or span needed\" note are declarative and permissive, with no conditional behavior to test.\n\n**Gaps.** I found none. A weaker point is that no test asserts `outgoing_edges(...).fingerprint` directly. The fingerprint is checked through the recorded dependencies, which the implementation builds from the snapshot's own fingerprint, so I don't count this as a gap.",
